<%# Admin sidebar navigation.

    Rows use NavHelper, the same treatment as the app nav, so moving between the two is not a
    change of scenery. Before this the app sidebar was a saturated teal panel and this one was
    white with no selected state at all - you could not tell which admin section you were in.

    nav_section_active? matches the section and anything below it, so a show or edit page keeps
    its parent row highlighted. aria-current carries that to assistive tech, since a tint and a
    bar are colour alone (WCAG 1.4.1). %>
<% sections = [
     { heading: nil,
       items: [{ label: "Dashboard", path: admin_root_path, icon: "house", exact: true }] },
     { heading: "Academic",
       items: [{ label: "Classrooms", path: admin_classrooms_path, icon: "presentation" },
               { label: "Schools", path: admin_schools_path, icon: "school" },
               { label: "School years", path: admin_school_years_path, icon: "calendar-check" }] },
     { heading: "Users",
       items: [{ label: "Students", path: admin_students_path, icon: "graduation-cap" },
               { label: "Teachers", path: admin_teachers_path, icon: "presentation" },
               { label: "Users", path: admin_users_path, icon: "users" }] },
     { heading: "Portfolio",
       items: [{ label: "Stocks", path: admin_stocks_path, icon: "chart-line" },
               { label: "Transactions", path: admin_portfolio_transactions_path,
                 icon: "arrow-left-right" }] },
     { heading: "Content",
       items: [{ label: "Announcements", path: admin_announcements_path, icon: "megaphone" }] }
   ] %>

<div class="space-y-4">
  <% sections.each do |section| %>
    <div>
      <% if section[:heading] %>
        <h2 class="px-3 mb-1 text-xs font-semibold text-slate-500"><%= section[:heading] %></h2>
      <% end %>

      <div class="space-y-1">
        <% section[:items].each do |item| %>
          <% active = nav_section_active?(item[:path], exact: item.fetch(:exact, false)) %>
          <%= link_to item[:path],
                      aria: { current: ("page" if active) },
                      class: nav_row_class(active: active) do %>
            <span class="<%= nav_indicator_class(active: active) %>" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            <%= lucide_icon(item[:icon], class: nav_icon_class(active: active)) %>
            <span><%= item[:label] %></span>
          <% end %>
        <% end %>
      </div>
    </div>
  <% end %>

  <%# The component demo is **not here**, and that is the second time this sidebar has run out of room.

      It was a `Development` section with one purple row. Two problems, and the second is why it moved
      rather than just losing the colour:

      **A hand-rolled row drifts.** It kept `min-h-11 py-2` with no `lg:` step after NavHelper moved a
      desktop row to 36px, so it rendered 44px against every neighbour's 36px. Nothing caught it, because
      the guard was `Rails.env.development?` and the suite runs in the test environment - the row was
      invisible to every test in this repo.

      **And it did not fit.** Measured on a 1366x768 Chromebook, the ten product rows are 561px in 561px of
      available height: exact, with no slack at all. The section cost 67px, so in development this sidebar
      has always scrolled, and `spacing_test`'s "fits a Chromebook" assertion passed only because it could
      not see the row. A non-product destination that does not fit the sidebar goes in the **top bar** -
      which is a decision this app already made, for `View site`, after a footer row here pushed the nav
      68px past the same viewport. Same problem, same answer, and it is in `layouts/admin` now.

      The purple is worth recording as its own mistake. It was explained as "deliberately off the shared
      treatment so it is obviously not a production destination", but the sidebar has one colour vocabulary
      and it means "you are here": measured, purple-700's chroma is 0.265 against slate-700's 0.044, so the
      idle demo row was about six times more saturated than the ten real rows and read louder than the
      *selected* row of the section you were actually in. Contrast was never the problem - 7.07:1 idle,
      5.54:1 on the icon, 6.58:1 on the active tint. Colour was the wrong instrument: a page can say "this
      is not the product" in a sentence, which is what the environment banner does. %>
</div>
